00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:29.189
White dwarfs pack the mass of the Sun into a volume the size of Earth,

00:04:29.609 --> 00:04:32.869
so their atmospheres are thousands of times denser than the Sun's.

00:04:33.269 --> 00:04:38.289
In ordinary stars, the light we see follows a smooth black-body curve set only by temperature,

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:42.849
However, the new models show that a few percent of white dwarves deviate from that curve.

00:04:43.429 --> 00:04:48.089
Add carbon at the same 6,000 Kelvin, and the tint turns pink or purple because the

00:04:48.089 --> 00:04:50.889
element absorbs and reamits light at specific bands.

00:04:51.988 --> 00:04:56.948
Models treat the stars as black bodies, whose emission depends only on temperature, yet the

00:04:56.948 --> 00:04:58.988
added elements shift the observed spectrum.

00:04:59.528 --> 00:05:04.248
Dense atmospheres allow trace elements to alter that pattern in ways impossible for main

00:05:04.248 --> 00:05:05.128
sequence stars.

00:05:05.128 --> 00:05:10.608
The same dense layers also trapped debris from destroyed planets, and green tones appear

00:05:10.608 --> 00:05:12.728
when that material settles into the atmosphere.

00:05:13.348 --> 00:05:18.688
Cooler white dwarfs with hydrogen and helium tend toward cyan or blue, because those gases

00:05:18.688 --> 00:05:20.848
suppress certain parts of the visible range.

00:05:21.508 --> 00:05:26.068
These effects only appear because the atmospheres are so compressed that even parts per million

00:05:26.068 --> 00:05:29.088
abundances of heavy elements become optically important.

00:05:29.608 --> 00:05:33.948
In a normal star, the same trace amounts would be invisible against the much lower density.

00:05:33.948 --> 00:05:39.208
The team's computer models treat the atmosphere as a stratified layer, where pressure, temperature,

00:05:39.608 --> 00:05:41.648
and composition interact at every depth.

00:05:42.248 --> 00:05:46.908
Small changes in the abundance of carbon can swing the output from pink to purple depending

00:05:46.908 --> 00:05:48.168
on the exact temperature.

00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:53.348
The five stars in the nearby sample already demonstrate that the phenomenon is repeatable

00:05:53.348 --> 00:05:54.928
rather than a modeling artifact.

00:05:55.448 --> 00:06:00.968
Because the color shift depends on both temperature and composition, observers can now use broadband

00:06:00.968 --> 00:06:06.888
to flag candidates for follow-up spectroscopy instead of examining every white dwarf in detail.

00:06:07.608 --> 00:06:12.228
Future observations with larger telescopes could measure the actual spectra of these objects

00:06:12.228 --> 00:06:15.128
and confirm whether the predicted colors match reality.

00:06:15.608 --> 00:06:20.688
If they do, color surveys could become an efficient first filter for identifying white dwarfs

00:06:20.688 --> 00:06:21.908
that once hosted planets.

00:06:22.608 --> 00:06:26.948
The remaining puzzle is whether these colored stars represent a brief transitional phase

00:06:26.948 --> 00:06:32.308
or a stable end state, and whether the same process operates in white dwarfs that form through

00:06:32.308 --> 00:06:37.448
different channels. We can already see the color difference in the models, but we do not yet know

00:06:37.448 --> 00:06:42.368
how often the responsible elements arrive from outside versus how often they are dredged up from

00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:47.808
the star's own interior. The open question is how long these color signatures persist before the

00:06:47.808 --> 00:06:53.828
elements sink or are mixed away. Some white dwarfs may show the effect only during a narrow window after

00:06:53.828 --> 00:06:58.968
a planetary disruption event. Others could maintain the color for much longer if the elements

00:06:58.968 --> 00:07:03.908
remain suspended. Resolving that time scale would tell us whether the colored population

00:07:03.908 --> 00:07:09.128
is a steady fraction of all white dwarfs, or a transient phase that most stars pass through.

